\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Protocol $\times $ head, Wilson 95\% intervals, \textbf  {deployment stack only}. The memorized head leads on \emph  {dev} and collapses on held-out and randomized; the released head is flat across all three. Our free per-tick regression baseline, same trunk and corpus regime, scores 0/56 (red rule). The audit-stack control is deliberately \emph  {not} plotted here --- see Figure~\ref {fig:auditstack}.}}{6}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:protocol}{{1}{6}{Protocol $\times $ head, Wilson 95\% intervals, \textbf {deployment stack only}. The memorized head leads on \emph {dev} and collapses on held-out and randomized; the released head is flat across all three. Our free per-tick regression baseline, same trunk and corpus regime, scores 0/56 (red rule). The audit-stack control is deliberately \emph {not} plotted here --- see Figure~\ref {fig:auditstack}}{figure.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Protocol $\times $ head, \textbf  {exploratory}: all cells $k/10$; task 0, wrist camera. Siblings A and B are the two training runs adjacent to the released head in the selection sweep (same corpus and objective, differing only in seed and epoch count), retained to show the pattern is not one run's accident. Audit-stack cells are never pooled with deployment-stack cells. \textbf  {Read the pattern, not the cells}: Wilson intervals at $n{=}10$ overlap almost everywhere (7/10 is $[0.40,0.89]$, 4/10 is $[0.17,0.69]$) and we do \emph  {not} test the head $\times $ protocol interaction the pattern suggests. Only the $n{=}50$ rows below are inferential.}}{6}{table.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
