\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The audit-stack control, on its own axes because it must not be read against Figure~\ref {fig:protocol}. Same protocol, same heads, same draws --- but a \emph  {rebuilt} detector stack, on which the separation \textbf  {inverts}: the memorized head scores 6/10 where the released head scores 0/10, the exact reverse of the deployment stack. This is why the behavioural certificate is joint in \emph  {(head, stack)} rather than a property of the head. An earlier draft drew this as an inset inside Figure~\ref {fig:protocol}, which invited precisely the cross-stack comparison the result forbids.}}{7}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:auditstack}{{2}{7}{The audit-stack control, on its own axes because it must not be read against Figure~\ref {fig:protocol}. Same protocol, same heads, same draws --- but a \emph {rebuilt} detector stack, on which the separation \textbf {inverts}: the memorized head scores 6/10 where the released head scores 0/10, the exact reverse of the deployment stack. This is why the behavioural certificate is joint in \emph {(head, stack)} rather than a property of the head. An earlier draft drew this as an inset inside Figure~\ref {fig:protocol}, which invited precisely the cross-stack comparison the result forbids}{figure.caption.14}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.2}Role binding: a mechanism we proposed, tested, and retracted}{7}{subsection.5.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:probes}{{5.2}{7}{Role binding: a mechanism we proposed, tested, and retracted}{subsection.5.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Substitution-probe attribution across heads: how far each head's predicted grasp point moves when one input is swapped between the two most-separated recorded episodes. The released head (v5) reads its \emph  {visual} channels (1.1--1.8\,cm) and barely moves on proprioception (0.09\,cm) --- the repair's signature. \textbf  {The counterexample that motivates pairing probes with behavioural randomization}: v2 and v2.1 have near-identical profiles on the jointly logged channels while scoring \textbf  {0.000 and 0.700} deployed. A probe evaluated on teacher trajectories is an on-manifold instrument and cannot, alone, certify off-manifold behaviour. (The two text blocks inside the plot area restate this caption and the v1 note; they are redundant with it and should be dropped when the figure is regenerated --- see the reproducibility note in \S  \ref {sec:limits}.) Hatched bars and $\times $ marks are channels not separately logged for that head, never zeros.}}{8}{figure.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:attribution}{{3}{8}{Substitution-probe attribution across heads: how far each head's predicted grasp point moves when one input is swapped between the two most-separated recorded episodes. The released head (v5) reads its \emph {visual} channels (1.1--1.8\,cm) and barely moves on proprioception (0.09\,cm) --- the repair's signature. \textbf {The counterexample that motivates pairing probes with behavioural randomization}: v2 and v2.1 have near-identical profiles on the jointly logged channels while scoring \textbf {0.000 and 0.700} deployed. A probe evaluated on teacher trajectories is an on-manifold instrument and cannot, alone, certify off-manifold behaviour. (The two text blocks inside the plot area restate this caption and the v1 note; they are redundant with it and should be dropped when the figure is regenerated --- see the reproducibility note in \S \ref {sec:limits}.) Hatched bars and $\times $ marks are channels not separately logged for that head, never zeros}{figure.caption.17}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.3}The blocked object: nine mechanisms, eight refuted}{8}{subsection.5.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:drift}{{5.3}{8}{The blocked object: nine mechanisms, eight refuted}{subsection.5.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Separating object identity from table position, ten objects, $n{=}50$ home-pose states each, audit stack. \textbf  {(A)} At shipped positions the two clusters do not separate: means 0.0418 (A) vs 0.0503 (B), exact Mann--Whitney $U{=}18$, $p{=}0.3095$; within-cluster spread is $3.2\times $ (A) and $4.8\times $ (B) the between-cluster difference. Distance from the training object's position predicts little (Spearman $+0.193$, exact permutation $p{=}0.5968$); detection confidence predicts a lot (Spearman $\mathbf  {-0.709}$, $p{=}\mathbf  {0.0268}$). The bracket marks the decisive pair --- 4.74\,mm apart, $2.42\times $ apart in gap. \textbf  {(B)} Each object teleported 22\,cm to the other cluster, identity held constant. The position account predicts A$\to $B rises and B$\to $A falls; measured, A$\to $B is $-0.0026$ and B$\to $A is $+0.0058$, both opposite, concordance 5/10, exact sign test $p{=}1.0000$. Spearman(native, swapped) $={+}0.709$, $p{=}0.0268$: the ordering travels with the object. Displacement is not inert (mean $|\Delta |{=}0.0066$) but is unsystematic and small beside the identity contrasts (cream$-$soup 0.0142, cream$-$butter 0.0414).}}{9}{figure.caption.18}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:posvsapp}{{4}{9}{Separating object identity from table position, ten objects, $n{=}50$ home-pose states each, audit stack. \textbf {(A)} At shipped positions the two clusters do not separate: means 0.0418 (A) vs 0.0503 (B), exact Mann--Whitney $U{=}18$, $p{=}0.3095$; within-cluster spread is $3.2\times $ (A) and $4.8\times $ (B) the between-cluster difference. Distance from the training object's position predicts little (Spearman $+0.193$, exact permutation $p{=}0.5968$); detection confidence predicts a lot (Spearman $\mathbf {-0.709}$, $p{=}\mathbf {0.0268}$). The bracket marks the decisive pair --- 4.74\,mm apart, $2.42\times $ apart in gap. \textbf {(B)} Each object teleported 22\,cm to the other cluster, identity held constant. The position account predicts A$\to $B rises and B$\to $A falls; measured, A$\to $B is $-0.0026$ and B$\to $A is $+0.0058$, both opposite, concordance 5/10, exact sign test $p{=}1.0000$. Spearman(native, swapped) $={+}0.709$, $p{=}0.0268$: the ordering travels with the object. Displacement is not inert (mean $|\Delta |{=}0.0066$) but is unsystematic and small beside the identity contrasts (cream$-$soup 0.0142, cream$-$butter 0.0414)}{figure.caption.18}{}}
